var e=`# CHAPTER 21 — It's Not That I Can't\r
\r
On the other side of the park, in the brush on the right, Jack was getting annoyed.\r
\r
He'd gone in too deep. It was his fault. Nobody could find him.\r
\r
— HEY! — With both hands around his mouth. — I'M RIGHT HERE, YOU ASSHOLES! RIGHT HERE!\r
\r
Finally they showed up. Seven. Four women and three men. Jack smiled, got up from the log where he was sitting and threw a few punches in the air to warm up.\r
\r
— Seven, huh? I bet most of them went after Kim. That's annoying. You shouldn't underestimate me like that. And the worst part is this fight is gonna go on Kim's tab. That makes me even more pissed off.\r
\r
A girl with a punk look came forward. Mohawk, safety pin in her ear, combat boots. She sized Jack up from head to toe.\r
\r
— Ah, sweetie. You're not much.\r
\r
She pressed a finger to his chest.\r
\r
Jack looked at the finger. Then at her.\r
\r
— Look. I'm not underestimating you. I hope you don't do that to me either. It'll be worse for you. And I'm warning you now: it doesn't matter who it is. Kid, young, old, man, woman. If you came to fight, you're going to get beat.\r
\r
She smiled. Pulled a knife with her left hand and pressed the blade to his neck.\r
\r
Jack didn't move.\r
\r
— I'll give you one more chance. I offered your boss a way to end this without anyone getting hurt. I offer you the same. Walk away now and nobody gets broken. This is the last chance before it really starts. Because the first rule of street fighting is that there are no rules.\r
\r
— You're crazy. — She laughed. — Seven against one. And me with a knife at your neck. It'll be quick.\r
\r
Jack smiled.\r
\r
— Quoting V for Vendetta: what you have is a knife and the hope of getting to use it. If you can't... it's my turn.\r
\r
She pressed the knife.\r
\r
Jack's hand closed around her wrist before the blade went in. It closed with a strength she didn't expect from a guy his size. The pain made her hand open on its own. The knife fell.\r
\r
She jumped back, startled by his speed.\r
\r
He didn't give her time to think.\r
\r
— Kim would be pissed if he were here. — Already moving. — But I'll go easy so I don't mess up that pretty face.\r
\r
The punch hit her solar plexus, mid-inhale. The air didn't go in. She was thrown backward, flying into her friends, and spat blood before falling into their arms. Out cold.\r
\r
Jack rolled the arm he'd used. Took two steps back. Made a hand gesture.\r
\r
— So? Who's next?\r
\r
---\r
\r
— Holy shit, man! — One of the guys. — You didn't even go easy because she's a girl! Are you crazy?\r
\r
— If you think I hit less hard because she's a lady, you come. I don't have time to waste.\r
\r
The guy made a signal. The six spread into a circle around Jack.\r
\r
*At least this way I finish everyone at once.*\r
\r
The first to come was the one with the chain. He wanted to use the chain's reach to avoid getting close. He spun and brought the chain down from above.\r
\r
Jack didn't dodge.\r
\r
He raised his bent left arm and let the chain hit. The chain wrapped around his forearm. Jack twisted his arm, wrapping it more. Took a step. Wrapped more. When it felt tight, he pulled.\r
\r
The guy came along, pulled by his own chain.\r
\r
And on the way he met Jack's right cross.\r
\r
The guy's face stopped where the punch was. His legs kept going. They left the ground. For a moment the guy was horizontal in the air, and then he was sprawled on the ground next to Jack, who hadn't taken another step.\r
\r
The other five looked at their friend on the ground.\r
\r
That was their mistake.\r
\r
---\r
\r
When they looked up, Jack was already off to the side. The chain had fallen to the ground, and his foot hooked it and lifted his leg in a wide arc, the chain sweeping the air in a half-moon. One of the girls jumped back to get away and fell on top of a friend. Two more on the ground.\r
\r
Three were left standing. Two girls and a boy, the boy in the middle.\r
\r
Jack spun around to get closer, his back to them. As he spun, the sole of his pivot foot scraped a rock and kicked it up behind him. Nobody saw.\r
\r
He was with his back to the boy in the middle, less than half a meter away. He bent at the waist and lowered his head. The boy's eyes went to the rock falling behind Jack.\r
\r
Jack came back hard. The back of his head hit the boy's face from back to front. At the same time, his left hand caught the rock in the air.\r
\r
The two girls were coming, one from each side.\r
\r
He threw the rock at one's face. Her head snapped back. The rock hit her face and ricocheted straight into the other's face. Both stopped their charge.\r
\r
Jack took advantage. He picked the chain up off the ground, spun it above his head and let out as much chain as he could. It wrapped around the neck of the boy in the middle, who was a lot taller than him.\r
\r
Jack turned his back to the boy, pressed back-to-back. He pulled the chain over his shoulder and bent forward.\r
\r
The boy went up over Jack's back, flipped in the air and was thrown in front of him.\r
\r
Between the lack of air and the fall, there was nothing that boy could do but pass out.\r
\r
---\r
\r
Jack stood still in the middle of everyone and raised his hand.\r
\r
For some reason, everyone stopped.\r
\r
— Before I finish. I don't like hitting women. It's not to put you down. There's one in our gang. But it's not something I'm comfortable with. Sorry about the rock to both of your faces. It was instinct. You were going to attack me. So nobody else gets hurt, can this fight end here?\r
\r
The girl who was on the ground shouted.\r
\r
— You son of a bitch! You think we're scared? You don't scare anybody! Our boss wants the money that was agreed on. And when she wants something, she gets it.\r
\r
— Not this time. — Jack said, with pity.\r
\r
The two girls behind him each drew a knife and jumped at him at the same time.\r
\r
Jack jumped back.\r
\r
That was all.\r
\r
The two met mid-jump, and the knives went into the side of each other's stomachs. They stood looking at each other, both with apologetic faces.\r
\r
— Sorry, I didn't mean to...\r
\r
Jack didn't let her finish. Kicked one's hand, the knife flew. Kicked the other's hand, the knife flew. While they watched the knives in the air, he reached them and trapped both by the neck, one in each arm, between his arm and forearm. He squeezed.\r
\r
When he felt their bodies give, he let go.\r
\r
The two went to the ground beside him.\r
\r
---\r
\r
Two were left.\r
\r
Jack crouched down slowly, knees bent, sitting on his own heels. He started drawing something in the dirt with his finger. He wasn't looking at them.\r
\r
— You know... — Nonchalant. — I'm not trying to scare you.\r
\r
He lifted his head slowly.\r
\r
From under the brim of his cap, you could only see one of his eyes. And that eye had changed. It wasn't the eye of the Jack who told jokes on the bus. It was a sunken eye. Of an animal hidden in the brush. Of someone who keeps something inside and locks it away. A cold eye that sent a chill down the spine of anyone who looked.\r
\r
— It's not that I can't. — In the same nonchalant voice. — I just don't want to.\r
\r
The two who were left didn't move.\r
\r
— So here's the thing. — Jack standing up, voice returning to normal as if nothing had happened. — Two options. Option A: you come here, we fight fair and square and you end up like everyone else. Option B: this bunch here needs a doctor. You grab your friends and vanish. A or B? And I'm serious.\r
\r
The two didn't need to think.\r
\r
They went straight to their friends on the ground, trying to wake one, lift another.\r
\r
Jack watched that for a second. Then he turned his back and walked off through the brush, lighting a cigarette, hands in his pockets.\r
\r
— GUYS! — Shouting to nowhere. — PEOPLE! HEY! IT'S ME, JACK! IF YOU'RE AROUND, YELL! I'M GOING TO KEEP WALKING SLOW AND SHOUTING. DON'T MAKE ME WALK TOO FAR, 'CAUSE I'M TIRED AND I STILL HAVE TO WORK!\r
`;export{e as default};
